Split out of #15, whose autoflush half is done. This is #15 point 2, unchanged:

lib/Net/Async/MCP/Server/Transport/Stdio.pm, _send_response:

    print { $self->write_handle } encode_json($response) . "\n";

The write is a blocking print, so a full stdout pipe blocks the event loop -
the same class of problem #1 fixed on the read side. Fixing it properly means
routing the write through the same IO::Async::Stream (write_handle => ...,
$stream->write(...)), which then needs a flush-before-return at EOF so a
response queued when stdin closes is not dropped.

Note the ordering constraint the autoflush fix does not remove: with the
write on the stream, "the response left the handle" becomes "the stream
drained it", so t/04-stdio-transport.t subtest "responses are flushed as they
are written" is the test that has to keep passing - it reads the far end of a
real pipe mid-run and would catch a response that only leaves at EOF.

